Data center cooling solutions are essential for removing heat generated by servers, storage systems, networking equipment, and other information technology infrastructure. Effective thermal management helps maintain equipment reliability, supports operational continuity, and improves the energy efficiency of data center facilities.

The market encompasses air-cooled systems, liquid-cooling technologies, coolant distribution units (CDUs), computer room air conditioners (CRACs), chillers, and related equipment. Operators increasingly evaluate integrated cooling architectures rather than individual components, considering factors such as rack density, power usage effectiveness (PUE), water consumption, maintenance requirements, and the ability to scale infrastructure.

The rapid deployment of AI accelerators and high-performance computing systems is increasing thermal-management requirements. Traditional air cooling remains important across many facilities, while direct-to-chip liquid cooling and other liquid-based approaches are gaining attention in high-density environments where concentrated heat loads can exceed the practical capabilities of conventional systems.

